    "searchText": "change http and openapi keep proctor routes, dtos, authentication requirements, public errors, authored yaml, generated json, and rendered api reference in agreement. change http and openapi proctor's public api has one human authoring system and several verified consumers. resource-oriented yaml modules under server/openapi own the wire contract and reference prose. the compiler produces server/openapi.json ; runtime agreement tests, the webapp type generator, and the documentation site consume that reviewed artifact. never edit server/openapi.json or generated endpoint pages by hand. find the existing owner search by path or stable operation id: choose the existing product-area directory and narrowest resource module. create another descriptive yaml file only when no existing resource is a natural owner. files are discovered recursively; there is no manifest to edit. definitions used by one resource stay in that resource file. promote a schema, parameter, request body, or response to a product-area shared.yaml only when several resources in that area consume it. the root shared.yaml is for genuinely cross-area definitions. implement one complete operation an operation change normally touches these surfaces together: 1. application capability — typed command or query and result. 2. http resource — strict request dto, explicit domain mapping, typed result, and allowlisted public errors. 3. route catalog — one explicit credential and assurance requirement. 4. openapi source — exact path, method, schema, prose, examples, and proctor extensions. 5. agreement tests — route, authentication, dto, success schema, and public error agreement. 6. consumer adapters — regenerate webapp api types or update authored api recipes when the public contract requires it. the http routing kernel is sealed before serving. add the resource to its explicit production catalog; do not register routes through initialization, expose router internals, or add a generic response-writer escape hatch. trace the sealed route catalog the production route path is intentionally searchable: the resource constructor declares typed paths, operations, credential requirements, allowed public errors, body limits, idempotency, and any named protocol-specific result. it retains only its narrow application capability. it never retains the complete http options, application facade, router, root store, platform, or concrete adapter. the catalog rejects duplicate normalized method/path shapes, unknown parameter kinds, invalid authentication requirements, and incomplete public-error mappings before the server can accept traffic. every entry point—including the websocket upgrade—appears in this catalog. keep handlers mechanical an ordinary handler should be easy to review in one pass: 1. decode a bounded request into a transport-owned dto; 2. parse canonical ids and other wire values; 3. map them explicitly into one typed application command or query; 4. call one application capability with the immutable invocation; and 5. map the safe result into a response dto. apply the body limit before decoding. json requests accept exactly one value, reject unknown fields and trailing content, and distinguish omission, zero, and explicit null with optional[t] when the contract needs that distinction. only a named bounded extension object may accept unknown keys. domain models never double as request or response dtos. an innocent field added to a domain record must not silently become a new public field. ordinary operations return a typed status/body result and error . redirects, uploads, downloads, and upgrade transfer use reviewed named result types. the websocket handshake is the sole raw-response exception because a successful upgrade transfers connection ownership to the sibling transport. declare credential and assurance separately every route declares what establishes its principal: public, authenticated, interactive session, refresh credential, strong authentication, recent reauthentication, or a composed requirement. administrative permission is not a route authentication class; the application use case evaluates it against the current resource. cookie-authenticated unsafe requests also require the route’s csrf proof. bearer and cookie sources cannot be mixed and resolved by precedence. keep the runtime declaration, openapi security , and x-proctor-auth value in exact agreement. map expected failures exhaustively an expected application failure uses a stable dotted code with explicitly safe fields. it contains no http status, localized prose, request id, sql detail, or stack trace. the transport maps only allowlisted codes to declared rfc 9457 problem details responses. unknown or undeclared errors become a generic correlated internal failure and are logged once at the outer operational boundary. never improvise a status from an error string. the owning resource’s agreement test proves its complete success schema and public-error set against openapi. read authorization, errors, and audit before deciding whether a denied exact resource is safely forbidden or concealed as not found. design collections and cursors new collections return an object with a non-null items array and optional next cursor . unbounded collections use keyset pagination, not growing offsets. cursors are opaque, versioned, bounded, and untrusted. clients return them unchanged. the shared envelope validates canonical encoding and object shape; the resource owns the keyset fields, stable ordering, semantic validation, and any compatibility window. never include a private workspace path, credential, or unbounded filter in a cursor. supply the full contract every operation has exactly one public product-area tag and all of the following: - concise imperative summary ; - substantive behavioral description ; - explicit openapi security ; - x-proctor-auth matching the runtime route; - x-proctor-error-codes matching declared responses; - x-proctor-idempotency set to none , optional , or required ; - a purpose description for every parameter and request body; and - a schema-valid synthetic request example for mutations, or an executable x-codesamples example for bodyless and multipart mutations. public failures use rfc 9457 problem details and stable application codes. unknown application errors fail closed; do not turn an undeclared internal error into an improvised public response. examples must never contain real institutions, users, exams, answers, credentials, email addresses, object keys, hostnames, or local paths. use clearly synthetic values and explicit credential placeholders. build and prove agreement regenerate the reviewed artifact: review the server/openapi.json diff, then validate source-to-artifact drift: prove agreement with the registered http surface: for a reference-content or presentation change, also run: finish with make docs-check when endpoint pages, product-area navigation, api recipes, or reference presentation changed. regenerate browser types when a changed schema is consumed by the hosted webapp: the generated typescript schema is a consumer of the reviewed openapi artifact. do not patch it to compensate for an incomplete source contract. preserve compatibility the current public api is versioned under /api/v1 . contract changes are additive unless a new api version and migration path are explicitly designed. renaming a field, narrowing an accepted value, changing concealment behavior, or removing a public error may be breaking even when go compilation succeeds. next - coordinate the owning server use case. - update the hosted webapp when it consumes the contract. - select the complete verification level."
